#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 540.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158190} :activity/id #uuid "84498d8d-76ea-5a50-9b08-d23d72f06fa2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049760 :topic/id #uuid "1186861c-e8c9-51b2-abb6-810d94fb018e" :topic/math-academy-id 2883} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158190 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158513 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we can divide polynomials quickly by cleverly manipulating a rational function.\n\nFor example, suppose we wish to divide $x$ by $x+2.$ Then we have the following rational expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{x+2}\n$$\n\nThe goal is to write this rational expression in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{x+2} = a + \\dfrac{b}{x+2}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are both constants. Here, $a$ is the quotient of the division, and $b$ is the remainder.\n\nTo manipulate the rational function into this form, we can add and subtract $2$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x}{x+2}=\\frac{x+2-2}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we expand and simplify the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2-2}{x+2} & =\\frac{x+2}{x+2}+\\frac{-2}{x+2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{-2}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=1$ and $b=-2,$ and we've reached the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x}{x+2} = 1 + \\dfrac{-2}{x+2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e14d3743-6a07-55cb-a072-713f84ccc04f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4667 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "3923860c-a467-5d59-85ef-826427c68143" :step/math-academy-id 28781 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158192}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158191 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158514 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we have to factor the numerator of a rational expression in order to reveal a copy of the denominator. For example, consider the following rational expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x}{x+1}\n$$\n\nIn order to write this rational expression in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x}{x+1} = a + \\dfrac{b}{x+1},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, we can add and subtract $1$ in parentheses in the numerator. This will reveal a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2x}{x+1} & =\\frac{2(x+1-1)}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)+2(-1)}{x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+1)-2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we expand and simplify the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2(x+1)-2}{x+1} & =\\frac{2(x+1)}{x+1}+\\frac{-2}{x+1} \\\\  & =2+\\frac{-2}{x+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=2$ and $b=-2,$ and we've reached the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2x}{x+1} = 2 + \\dfrac{-2}{x+1}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "cd4e7a31-3d51-5105-8a86-30da71512b64" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4668 :tutorial/title "Manipulations Requiring Factoring"} :step/id #uuid "849a6fd2-d30f-5049-94e8-a62be750721f" :step/math-academy-id 28782 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158194}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158192 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157330 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157144 :question/id #uuid "465cc593-e81f-5040-bc49-e42b16c0b351" :question/math-academy-id "e-7720"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2331a0df-036e-55aa-adf7-ce24929b4efd" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048957}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157916} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558506} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558507}] :knowledge-point/title "Manipulating Rational Expressions With Single-Term Numerators"} :step/id #uuid "acddfddf-efe2-5255-98fc-4cff62292e97" :step/math-academy-id 28783 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158197}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158193 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157331 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157145 :question/id #uuid "0efff2c3-2e5e-5ba3-815d-8419940a5090" :question/math-academy-id "e-7721"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "48968893-6430-5e1b-ac7a-bd36991f910f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157917} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558493} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558495} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558503} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558516}] :knowledge-point/title "Manipulating Rational Expressions With Two-Term Numerators"} :step/id #uuid "1086b636-d9d0-5c61-aa9a-500d5ac6cb0a" :step/math-academy-id 28784 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158191}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158194 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157332 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157146 :question/id #uuid "79c2c218-2d4c-5e14-a60e-5f5cba22df4d" :question/math-academy-id "e-7722"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "81690612-bbae-5d54-a65c-f280d627e051" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157915} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157918} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558482} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558548} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186558556}] :knowledge-point/title "Manipulating Rational Expressions With Single-Term Numerators When Factoring Is Required"} :step/id #uuid "e90f9e32-d448-5c4c-9551-4ed1246b9042" :step/math-academy-id 28785} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158197 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158517 :tutorial/content "We can use the same method to manipulate rational expressions with two terms in the numerator. For example, consider the following rational expression:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+5}{x+2}\n$$\n\nIn order to write this rational expression in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+5}{x+2} = a + \\dfrac{b}{x+2}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, we can apply the same trick: add and subtract $2$ in the numerator. This reveals a copy of the denominator without changing the value of the expression.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+5}{x+2}=\\frac{x+2-2+5}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we expand and simplify the fraction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+2-2+5}{x+2} & =\\frac{x+2}{x+2}+\\frac{-2+5}{x+2} \\\\  & =1+\\frac{3}{x+2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we have $a=1$ and $b=3,$ and we've reached the desired result:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x+5}{x+2} = 1 + \\dfrac{3}{x+2}\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "a9c2d9fd-3261-5933-a02e-849f6acbb9e2" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4671 :tutorial/title "Manipulations With Two-Term Numerators"} :step/id #uuid "abcad088-58b0-5d3a-bb3b-9918bf529b03" :step/math-academy-id 28790 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158193}}] :activity/title "Dividing Polynomials by Manipulating Rational Expressions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157507}]}
